            <ArticleTitle>[Segmental renal infarction presenting as acute flank pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>756-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the clinical case of a male with acute onset right flank pain and significant deterioration of his general status.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">40-year-old male patient with acute onset of flank pain, progressive worsening, and fever up to 40 degrees C. Blood analysis (hemogram, coagulation tests, and serum ions), urine analysis, and abdominal x-rays, ultrasound and CT scan were performed. After the diagnosis of segmental renal infarction treatment with low molecular weight heparin was started.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patient improved clinically and was discharged from hospital 10 days later and received final discharge as outpatient after 6-month follow-up visit.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Renal infarction should be taken into consideration in the differential diagnosis of acute flank pain, when more frequent causes as renal colic or inflammatory-infectious diseases are ruled out, mainly in patients with cardiovascular diseases because the most frequent cause is embolism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
